[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   141 (so the test is not vacuously comparing 0 to 0).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors confirmed to already agree
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were candidate mismatches that testing showed the translation already
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduces. Recorded so a later reader does not have to rediscover them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Input failure**: EOF, `/dev/null` stdin, a closed stdin fd, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whitespace-only input all leave `x` at its initializer `0`, printing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `00000000`. `scanf` does not write to the destination on failure and the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program ignores the return value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Matching failure**: a first non-whitespace byte that cannot start a number
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`abc`, `.`, `-`, `+`, `--5`, `-  5`, …) also leaves `x` at `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Whitespace class**: `%d` skips exactly the `isspace` set
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`' ' \t \n \v \f \r`) and nothing else — a UTF-8 thin space is a matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   failure, not whitespace.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Truncation past `int`**: glibc converts `%d` into a `long` and stores the low
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes, so `2147483648` prints `00000080` and `4294967296` prints `00000000`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rather than saturating at `INT_MAX`. The Rust model reproduces this with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `value as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Clamping past `long`**: on range error glibc yields `LONG_MAX` / `LONG_MIN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   before the store, so `9223372036854775808` and `"9"*10000` both print
